<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WHAT FAILS THE BUILD.

  This is an INCLUDE filter: SpotBugs reports only what matches here, so the
  build gate is exactly these categories and nothing else.

  WHY IT IS SCOPED RATHER THAN "everything". A full run on this codebase
  reports 332 findings, and 306 of them are EI_EXPOSE_REP / EI_EXPOSE_REP2 -
  "may expose internal representation by returning a reference to a mutable
  object". That pattern is endemic to JPA entities, whose whole job is to hand
  out references to mutable state, and to the java.time and BigDecimal fields
  the entities carry. Gating on the full set would fail every build on day one
  and the gate would be switched off within a week. A gate that is always red
  is the same as no gate.

  WHY THESE THREE CATEGORIES. They are the ones that describe code that does
  not do what it says. The bug that prompted this file was
  EC_UNRELATED_TYPES - a String compared to an enum with .equals(), always
  false, which meant every delivery rider was told to collect nothing on a
  cash-on-delivery order. Three passes of manual review read past it; it is
  CORRECTNESS, so this gate would have stopped it at the build.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT GATED, and why it is not simply laziness:

    STYLE (15)        - 10 of them are RV_RETURN_VALUE_IGNORED_NO_SIDE_EFFECT
                        on deliberate Hibernate lazy-proxy initialisation
                        (calling a getter to force the proxy open while the
                        session still is, before the entity detaches and is
                        used after commit). SpotBugs cannot see that the proxy
                        initialisation IS the side effect, so it is wrong
                        about these, and gating on STYLE would make the fix be
                        "stop initialising the proxy" - a real bug traded for
                        a clean report.
    BAD_PRACTICE (6)  - all CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW, on services that validate
                        their configuration in the constructor. Failing fast
                        at startup on a missing JWT secret is the behaviour we
                        want, not a defect to design away.
    PERFORMANCE (3)   - BX_UNBOXING_IMMEDIATELY_REBOXED. Real, and worth about
                        nothing on a request path that also talks to Postgres.
    MALICIOUS_CODE    - the 306 above.

  These stay VISIBLE: `mvn com.github.spotbugs:spotbugs-maven-plugin:spotbugs`
  from the command line has no include filter and still reports all 332, so
  the wider set can be triaged deliberately. This file only decides what is
  allowed to break a build.

  RAISING THE BAR LATER is a matter of deleting an exclusion here once the
  matching findings are at zero - not of rewriting the pipeline.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <!-- Code that cannot do what it appears to do: always-false comparisons,
         null dereferences on a reachable path, ignored return values that
         genuinely have no effect, format-string mismatches. -->
    <Match>
        <Bug category="CORRECTNESS"/>
    </Match>

    <!-- Races and lock mistakes. Zero of these today, and the cost of a
         regression here is the kind of bug that only shows up under real
         load with real money moving. -->
    <Match>
        <Bug category="MT_CORRECTNESS"/>
    </Match>

    <!-- Injection and friends. Zero today. -->
    <Match>
        <Bug category="SECURITY"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
